    "result_text": "Build is green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for the E.164 telephone.\n\n**The key constraint:** the seed owners (e.g. `6085551023`) are 10-digit US numbers stored without a country code. On every create, the controller normalizes each existing owner's telephone through `TelephoneNormalizer.normalize(...)` for duplicate detection. Under the default `+61` country code those legacy numbers become `+61` + 10 national digits \u2014 which violates the new `+61`=9 rule. If I'd put the strict check inside the shared `normalize` method, it would throw during comparison and break *every* create. So the strict check must apply only to the client-supplied create value, not to canonicalization of already-stored legacy data.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. `TelephoneNormalizer.java`\n   - Added `NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map (`\"1\"`\u219210, `\"61\"`\u21929). E.164 country codes are prefix-free, so at most one is a prefix of any number.\n   - Added `normalizeAndValidate(String)`, which normalizes to E.164 (reusing `normalize`) and then enforces the national-number length for the recognised country code, throwing `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) on a mismatch. Unknown country codes stay governed only by the generic 8\u201315 digit rule.\n   - Kept plain `normalize(String)` lenient so it can still canonicalize legacy stored values for duplicate comparison.\n\n2. `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`\n   - `normalizeOwnerFields` now calls `normalizeAndValidate(...)` for the incoming telephone so wrong-length values are rejected with 400 on create.\n\nThe provided acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`) passes, and the validating path also correctly rejects in-range-but-wrong-length inputs like `+61 12345678` (8 national digits) while accepting valid `+61` (9) and `+1` (10) numbers.",
